EMTG Regression Test System (Testatron)
========================================

This folder contains the EMTG regression test system, called "Testatron".
Testatron runs EMTG on a set of test cases and compares the results against
baseline (.emtg) truth files to detect regressions.

=============
PREREQUISITES
=============

Before running Testatron, you must obtain certain external data files that are
not included in this repository. See the sections below for details.

1. SPICE Ephemeris Files
   Required files are listed in: universe/ephemeris_files/go_get_these_files.txt
   These must be placed in: universe/ephemeris_files/

2. Launch Vehicle Library Files
   Required files are listed in: HardwareModels/go_get_these_files.txt
   These must be placed in: HardwareModels/

3. EMTG Executable
   You must build EMTG from source. See /docs/build_system/ for build guides.

4. PyEMTG
   The PyEMTG Python package is required. It is located at /PyEMTG/.

=====
USAGE
=====

From the testatron directory, run:

  python testatron.py [options]

Key options:
  -e <path>    Path to EMTG executable (default: c:/emtg/bin/EMTGv9.exe)
  -p <path>    Path to PyEMTG (default: c:/emtg/PyEMTG/)
  -a           Run all tests (default if no option specified)
  -u           Run unit/feature tests only
  -m           Run mission tests only
  -c <cases>   Run specific test cases (space-separated, no extension)
  -f <folders> Run all cases in specified test folder(s)
  --failure <path>  Re-run previously failed tests from a failed_tests.csv
  --update_truths   Re-run all tests and overwrite truth .emtg files

For full help:
  python testatron.py -h

Example (Linux):
  python testatron.py -e /path/to/EMTGv9 -p /path/to/PyEMTG/ -u

================
FOLDER STRUCTURE
================

testatron/
|-- testatron.py              Main test driver script
|-- README                    This file
|
|-- HardwareModels/           Hardware model configuration files
|   |-- default.emtg_launchvehicleopt    Default launch vehicle library
|   |-- default.emtg_spacecraftopt       Default spacecraft configuration
|   |-- default.emtg_powersystemsopt     Default power system specifications
|   |-- default.emtg_propulsionsystemopt Default propulsion system specs
|   |-- default_2stage.emtg_spacecraftopt Two-stage spacecraft configuration
|   |-- empty.ThrottleTable              Empty throttle table
|   `-- go_get_these_files.txt           Instructions for obtaining external
|                                        launch vehicle library files
|
|-- tests/                    Test cases organized by category
|   |-- global_mission_options/   Tests for global mission configuration
|   |-- journey_options/          Tests for journey-specific options
|   |-- mission_tests/            Mission-level tests (place tests here)
|   |-- output_options/           Tests for output configuration
|   |-- physics_options/          Tests for physics model options
|   |-- script_constraint_tests/  Tests for constraint scripting
|   |-- solver_options/           Tests for solver configuration
|   |-- spacecraft_options/       Tests for spacecraft hardware options
|   |-- state_representation_tests/  Tests for state representation methods
|   `-- transcription_tests/     Tests for transcription methods
|
|-- universe/                 Universe configuration and supporting data
|   |-- *.emtg_universe           Celestial body definition files
|   |-- atmosphere_files/         Atmospheric density model files
|   |-- gravity_files/            Gravity field model files
|   `-- ephemeris_files/          SPICE kernel files (must be obtained separately;
|                                 see ephemeris_files/go_get_these_files.txt)
|
`-- peatsa_test/              Parametric study (PEATSA) test files
    |-- PEATSATest.py             PEATSA test validation script
    |-- peatsa_test.emtgopt        PEATSA configuration file
    |-- peatsa_test_input.csv      Input parameter definitions
    `-- peatsa_test_input.py       Input script

==========
TEST FILES
==========

Each test case consists of two files with the same base name:
  <testname>.emtgopt   Mission options file (input to EMTG)
  <testname>.emtg      Baseline truth file (expected output for comparison)

Testatron automatically overrides the following paths in each .emtgopt file
at runtime so that tests are portable across different installations:
  - universe_folder  -> testatron/universe/
  - HardwarePath     -> testatron/HardwareModels/
  - gravity file paths -> testatron/universe/gravity_files/

===================
UNIVERSE DATA FILES
===================

The universe/ folder contains celestial body definitions for the following:
  Earth, Moon, Mars, Venus, Jupiter, Saturn, Sun, SmallBody, and variants.

Atmosphere files (universe/atmosphere_files/):
  EarthExponentialSMEMean.emtg_densityopt and related files.

Gravity files (universe/gravity_files/):
  JGM3.grv (Earth), LP150Q.grv (Moon), and others.

Ephemeris files (universe/ephemeris_files/):
  SPICE kernels must be downloaded separately. See go_get_these_files.txt
  in that folder for the list of required files and where to obtain them.

======================
HARDWARE MODEL FILES
======================

The HardwareModels/ folder contains hardware configuration files used by tests.
Some tests reference launch vehicle library files that are not included in this
repository because they contain data from the NASA Launch Services Program (NLSP)
National Launch Schedule (NLS-II). See HardwareModels/go_get_these_files.txt
for details on which files are needed and how to obtain them.

The default.emtg_launchvehicleopt file included in this repository contains
three placeholder entries:
  Fixed_Initial_Mass               - Sets a fixed initial spacecraft mass
  Depart_With_Spacecraft_Thrusters - Departs using onboard propulsion
  ExampleRocket                    - Example polynomial launch vehicle model

============================
TEST RESULTS AND OUTPUT
============================

Test output is written to: testatron/output/<timestamp>/
  test_results.csv    Summary of all tests run and their pass/fail status
  failed_tests.csv    Details of any failures (can be used with --failure option)
  <test>_comparison.csv  Per-test comparison details

If a test fails, the failed_tests.csv records which parameters did not match
the baseline truth file, along with the actual and expected values.
